The movement's peak period was from 1903 to 1932, during which time it produced a wide range of innovative and influential designs in various fields, including ceramics, fashion, silver, furniture, and graphic arts. The movement's influence can be seen in later styles such as Bauhaus and Art Deco.

In terms of broader cultural and artistic trends, the Wiener Werkstätte movement was part of the larger Arts and Crafts movement, which emphasized the importance of hand-made craftsmanship and the unity of the arts. The movement was also influenced by the Deutscher Werkbund (German Association of Craftsmen), which was formed in 1907 to promote the global competitiveness of German businesses and improve the quality of industrial design.

