Explain the context and significance of the Rostker v. Goldberg case in relation to the US armed forces' personnel selection process.
The Rostker v. Goldberg case was a landmark decision by the United States Supreme Court in 1981, which held that the practice of requiring only men to register for the draft was constitutional. The case centered around the Military Selective Service Act (MSSA), which had been enacted by Congress to apply only to men. A group of individuals, including Robert Goldberg, challenged the gender distinction as unconstitutional, arguing that it violated the Due Process Clause of the 5th Amendment.

The significance of the Rostker v. Goldberg case lies in its impact on the US armed forces' personnel selection process. The decision effectively upheld the existing policy of excluding women from military service, as the Army and Marine Corps had a policy of not allowing women in combat positions. The Supreme Court's ruling was based on the argument that Congress had not concluded that every position in the military, including non-combat roles, must be filled with combat-ready men.

In essence, the Rostker v. Goldberg case legitimized the existing gender-based distinction in the US military's personnel selection process, which has had lasting implications for women's participation in the military. The decision has been criticized for perpetuating gender-based discrimination and limiting women's opportunities for military service. However, it has also been seen as a reflection of the societal attitudes and norms of the time, which viewed military service as a male-dominated domain.
