Retrieve information on the Bem Sex-Role Inventory (BSRI) and its relevance to gender roles and masculinity/femininity.
The Bem Sex-Role Inventory (BSRI) is a measure of gender expression and gender roles, specifically assessing how people identify themselves psychologically in terms of masculinity and femininity. It was developed by Sandra Bem to examine psychological androgyny and provide empirical evidence for the advantage of a shared masculine and feminine personality versus a sex-typed categorization. The BSRI consists of 60 different personality traits, rated on a 7-point Likert scale, with 20 masculine, 20 feminine, and 20 filler traits thought to be gender neutral. The inventory is used to classify individuals into one of four gender role orientations: masculine, feminine, androgynous, or undifferentiated. The BSRI has been found to be empirically sound, with high test-retest reliability and internal consistency. It is widely used as a measure of gender roles and has been influential in the study of androgyny.
